+# Daily Grind security audit
+
+The site is static HTML with CSS and one HTTPS image from images.unsplash.com.
+There are no client-side scripts, third-party libraries, DOM insertion sinks,
+forms, cookies, or authentication implementation in this repository. No existing
+XSS sink was found; these protections reduce the impact of future injection.
+
+## Findings and changes
+
+- The server previously sent no browser security headers. Every response now has
+  CSP, anti-framing, MIME sniffing prevention, no-referrer, restricted browser
+  feature permissions, and no-store headers.
+- Inline styles prevented a restrictive style policy. Styles now live in
+  `/styles.css`, with the heading using a class. The page appearance is preserved.
+- Scripts, plugins, base URL changes, and framing are blocked immediately. The
+  stricter candidate also denies unspecified resources, connections, forms,
+  workers, and frames. It allows only same-origin CSS and images plus the existing
+  Unsplash image origin, without wildcards or unsafe script/style allowances.
+- The request handler uses a fixed URL base and explicit asset paths, avoiding
+  reliance on the untrusted Host header or request-derived filesystem paths.
+- CSP reports have a 16 KiB body limit, a fixed content type, and directive
+  validation. Logging includes only directive and disposition; URLs and source
+  samples are discarded. Reports are untrusted telemetry, never proof of an attack.
+
+## Rollout
+
+1. Deploy normally with `CSP_MODE=report-only` (the default). The baseline CSP is
+   enforced while the complete candidate is sent in
+   `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only`. Violations go to `/csp-report` and produce
+   a sanitized `CSP violation` server log entry. Ensure the reverse proxy forwards
+   this POST endpoint and does not strip response headers. Apply request size and
+   rate limits at the edge for this public telemetry endpoint.
+2. Review telemetry across representative production routes and traffic before
+   promotion. Missing reports alone do not establish compatibility: blockers and
+   unsupported browsers can suppress reporting. Investigate unexpected origins
+   rather than adding broad allowlists.
+3. Set `CSP_MODE=enforce` and restart through the normal deployment mechanism to
+   enforce the candidate. Revert to `report-only` to roll back the candidate;
+   baseline protections remain. Invalid mode values fail startup.
+
+Policies use HTTP response headers because report-only CSP and frame-ancestors
+cannot be deployed effectively through HTML meta tags. See the
+[MDN CSP reference](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy)
+and [report-only documentation](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only).
+
+## Deployment boundaries and validation
+
+- Terminate HTTPS at the deployment edge. Configure HSTS there after verifying
+  HTTPS coverage; this plain HTTP application does not trust forwarded protocol
+  headers or assume all subdomains are HTTPS-ready.
+- If serving the files with another host instead of `server.js`, replicate these
+  response headers and the report receiver there. HTML alone cannot provide them.
+- `/logout` remains the original placeholder response and does not invalidate a
+  session. Before adding authentication, implement a CSRF-protected state-changing
+  logout endpoint and secure session cookies. Current navigation routes retain
+  the original homepage fallback behavior.
+- No JavaScript was added merely to enforce policy. New scripts, forms, fonts,
+  remote resources, or dynamic styles require a policy review and staged rollout.
+- Run `npm test` for in-memory response, policy, markup, and reporting checks;
+  `npm run build` checks syntax. Neither command starts a server or imports its
+  entrypoint. These checks do not verify actual edge headers or browser behavior.
